1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,640
Monday.

2
00:00:01,100 --> 00:00:02,700
The school computer lab, after dark.

3
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:05,680
Kai has hacked nothing, but has borrowed every

4
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:06,040
screen.

5
00:00:06,520 --> 00:00:07,400
The rock is still coming.

6
00:00:07,680 --> 00:00:08,740
But now you have a plan.

7
00:00:09,280 --> 00:00:10,760
You can't catch it, Kai says.

8
00:00:11,340 --> 00:00:11,940
Bo looks offended.

9
00:00:12,660 --> 00:00:14,120
So what if you had to stop an

10
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:14,560
asteroid?

11
00:00:15,160 --> 00:00:15,660
The real way.

12
00:00:16,860 --> 00:00:17,460
Think about it.

13
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:19,200
The rock weighs about three and a half

14
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:22,220
billion kilograms, and it moves twenty kilometres every

15
00:00:22,220 --> 00:00:22,580
second.

16
00:00:23,060 --> 00:00:25,120
Catching it means stopping all that energy at

17
00:00:25,120 --> 00:00:25,380
once.

18
00:00:25,700 --> 00:00:27,320
About a hundred and seventy megatons.

19
00:00:27,320 --> 00:00:28,860
Bo's arms are strong.

20
00:00:29,100 --> 00:00:31,160
But a hundred and seventy megatons would turn

21
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:34,080
Bo, the rock, and the town into vapour.

22
00:00:34,080 --> 00:00:35,520
So the trick isn't stopping it.

23
00:00:35,660 --> 00:00:37,820
It's changing where it goes, a tiny bit,

24
00:00:37,820 --> 00:00:38,880
very early.

25
00:00:40,340 --> 00:00:41,040
Here's the magic.

26
00:00:41,640 --> 00:00:44,240
Change a rock's speed by one centimetre per

27
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:44,600
second.

28
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:45,980
Much slower than walking.

29
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:48,300
Do it ten years early, and that tiny

30
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:50,720
push adds up to thousands of kilometres by

31
00:00:50,720 --> 00:00:51,300
the time it arrives.

32
00:00:51,700 --> 00:00:52,720
Enough to miss the whole planet.

33
00:00:52,720 --> 00:00:55,280
It's like steering a huge ship.

34
00:00:55,660 --> 00:00:57,720
Turn the wheel early, and a tiny turn

35
00:00:57,720 --> 00:00:58,260
is enough.

36
00:00:58,640 --> 00:01:01,240
Turn it late, and nothing can save you.

37
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:03,320
Push it late, and you need a giant

38
00:01:03,320 --> 00:01:03,700
shove.

39
00:01:04,080 --> 00:01:06,580
Push it early, and a nudge is enough.

40
00:01:07,760 --> 00:01:09,640
That's exactly what NASA tested.

41
00:01:10,040 --> 00:01:13,800
In 2022, the DART spacecraft flew into Dimorphos,

42
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:15,380
a small moon of a bigger asteroid.

43
00:01:15,760 --> 00:01:18,600
It hit at twenty-two thousand kilometres an hour.

44
00:01:19,020 --> 00:01:21,300
The moon's orbit got thirty-two minutes shorter.

45
00:01:21,300 --> 00:01:24,300
NASA had hoped for seventy-three seconds.

46
00:01:24,300 --> 00:01:26,760
And the rubble it blasted off pushed the

47
00:01:26,760 --> 00:01:27,440
rock even more.

48
00:01:28,020 --> 00:01:30,500
Now a European spacecraft called Hera is on

49
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:32,100
its way to see the crash site up

50
00:01:32,100 --> 00:01:32,460
close.

51
00:01:33,880 --> 00:01:35,460
Scientists have a gentler idea too.

52
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:38,260
A heavy spacecraft parks next to the asteroid.

53
00:01:38,600 --> 00:01:39,500
It doesn't touch it.

54
00:01:39,840 --> 00:01:42,100
Its own gravity slowly tugs the rock off

55
00:01:42,100 --> 00:01:42,360
course.

56
00:01:42,820 --> 00:01:44,120
It's called a gravity tractor.

57
00:01:44,740 --> 00:01:45,500
It's slow.

58
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:47,300
But with enough years, it works.

59
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:50,700
That night, the five of you lie on

60
00:01:50,700 --> 00:01:51,700
the school football pitch.

61
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:53,940
Nia holds Ravi's hand so he doesn't float

62
00:01:53,940 --> 00:01:54,260
away.

63
00:01:54,820 --> 00:01:55,700
Bo holds Nia's.

64
00:01:56,180 --> 00:01:56,960
Kai counts down.

65
00:01:57,760 --> 00:01:59,380
Ravi raises one hand towards the sky.

66
00:01:59,380 --> 00:02:01,900
And you stop time.

67
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:04,240
For everyone else, it's one second.

68
00:02:04,560 --> 00:02:06,700
For Ravi, it's a whole hour of gentle

69
00:02:06,700 --> 00:02:07,120
pushing.

70
00:02:07,120 --> 00:02:09,240
Like a gravity tractor with headphones.

71
00:02:09,980 --> 00:02:10,600
Time starts again.

72
00:02:11,140 --> 00:02:12,700
The bright dot in the sky doesn't look

73
00:02:12,700 --> 00:02:13,040
different.

74
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:15,160
But Kai is checking the numbers.

75
00:02:15,820 --> 00:02:16,360
Then Kai smiles.

76
00:02:17,140 --> 00:02:17,700
It's going to miss.

77
00:02:18,520 --> 00:02:20,480
A few weeks later, the rock flies past

78
00:02:20,480 --> 00:02:20,760
Earth.

79
00:02:21,300 --> 00:02:23,100
Millions of people watch it cross the sky.

80
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:26,060
Nobody knows that five teenagers moved it.

81
00:02:26,860 --> 00:02:28,700
Kai finally opens the crisps.

82
00:02:29,640 --> 00:02:31,680
So which power would you bring to save

83
00:02:31,680 --> 00:02:32,060
the planet?

84
00:02:32,520 --> 00:02:33,120
Tell us in the comments.

85
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:35,880
And subscribe, because the five of you are

86
00:02:35,880 --> 00:02:36,820
just getting started.
